# {{non-gloss|Senses relating to [[exert]]ing [[force#Noun|force]] or [[pull#Verb|pulling]].}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|often|formal}} To pull (someone or something) in a [[particular#Adjective|particular]] [[direction]] or [[manner#Noun|manner]]. {{defdate|from 8th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|He '''drew''' a sheaf of papers from his bag.}}
##* {{RQ:Burroughs Land That Time Forgot|chapter=VIII|passage=Lys shuddered, and I put my arm around her and '''drew''' her to me; and thus we sat throughout the hot night. She told me of her abduction and of the fright she had undergone, and together we thanked God that she had come through unharmed, because the great brute had dared not pause along the danger-infested way.}}
##* {{RQ:Orwell Animal Farm|chapter=1|passage=At the last moment Mollie, the foolish, pretty white mare who '''drew''' Mr. Jones's trap, came mincing daintily in, chewing at a lump of sugar.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[move#Verb|move]] (a [[body part]]) in a particular direction. {{defdate|from 13th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|She settled in the window seat, '''drawing''' her leg up beneath her.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} ''Often followed by'' '''[[tight#Adjective|tight]]''': to pull (something, such as a [[belt#Noun|belt]] or [[string#Noun|string]]) so that it [[tighten]]s or [[wrap#Verb|wraps]] around something more [[closely]]. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|She took a deep breath and '''drew''' her corset-strings.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[drag#Verb|drag]] (something), especially along the [[ground#Noun|ground]].
##* {{RQ:Dampier New Voyage|passage=Seals {{...}} throw their bodies forward, '''drawing''' their hinder-parts after them.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To pull ([[blind#Noun|blinds]], a [[curtain#Noun|curtain]], etc.) [[open#Adjective|open]] or [[closed#Adjective|closed]]. {{defdate|from 13th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|She '''drew''' the curtains to let in the sunlight.}}
##: {{ux|en|You should '''draw''' the curtains at night for privacy.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|year=1944|month=November and December|title="Duplex Roomette" Sleeping Cars|journal=Railway Magazine|page=324|passage=It is realised that the old Pullman standard sleeper, with its convertible "sections", each containing upper and lower berths, and with no greater privacy at night than the curtains '''drawn''' along both sides of a middle aisle, has had its day.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To pull out (a [[bolt#Noun|bolt]] or [[latch#Noun|latch]]) to [[unlock#Verb|unlock]] a [[door#Noun|door]], [[gate#Noun|gate]], etc.; also, to [[push#Verb|push]] in (a bolt or latch) to [[lock#Verb|lock]] a door, gate, etc.
## {{lb|en|transitive}} ''Chiefly followed by'' '''[[aside#Adverb|aside]]''' ''or'' '''to one [[side#Noun|side]]''': to move (someone) away from a [[group#Noun|group]] of people in order to [[speak#Verb|speak]] to them [[privately]].
## {{lb|en|transitive|reflexive}} To [[assume]] a [[specific#Adjective|specific]] [[attitude#Noun|attitude]] or [[position#Noun|position]], either by pulling in or [[stretch#Verb|stretching]] out one's [[body#Noun|body]] or [[limb#Noun|limbs]]. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|He '''drew''' himself to his full height and glowered at the interloper.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|figurative}}
### To [[cause#Verb|cause]] (a body part) to [[contract#Verb|contract]] or [[shrink#Verb|shrink]]; also, to pull (the [[mouth#Noun|mouth]], the [[face#Noun|face]] or [[feature#Noun|features]], etc.) [[out of shape]] from [[emotion]], etc.; to [[distort]]. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
###* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 8|act=IV|scene=ii|passage=How long her face is '''drawn'''!}}
### {{lb|en|archaic}} To cause (someone or something) to [[go#Verb|go]] from one [[place#Noun|place]] to another, or from one [[condition#Noun|condition]] to another.
### {{lb|en|archaic}} To [[construct#Verb|construct]] (a [[canal#Noun|canal]], [[wall#Noun|wall]], etc.) from one [[point#Noun|point]] to another. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
###* {{quote-book|en|year=1874|author=w:John Richard Green|title=w:A Short History of the English People|passage=the huge Offa's dike which he '''drew''' from the mouth of Wye to that of Dee}}
### {{lb|en|billiards}} To [[strike#Verb|strike]] (the [[cue ball]]) below the [[center#Noun|centre]] so as to [[give#Verb|give]] it a [[backward#Preposition|backward]] [[rotation]] which causes it to move backwards on striking another [[ball#Noun|ball]]. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
### {{lb|en|bowls}} To cause (a [[bowl#Noun|bowl]]) to move in a [[curve#Noun|curve]] to a certain place.
### {{lb|en|cricket|archaic}} Of a [[batter#Noun|batter]]: to [[hit#Verb|hit]] (a ball) from the [[off side]] to the [[leg side]], especially with an [[inclined#Adjective|inclined]] [[bat#Noun|bat]]; also, to hit (a [[short#Adjective|short]]-[[length#Noun|length]] ball [[direct#Verb|directed]] at the [[leg stump]]) with an inclined bat so as to [[deflect]] the ball between the [[leg#Noun|legs]] and the [[wicket]]. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
### {{lb|en|curling}} To [[play#Verb|play]] (a [[shot#Noun|shot]] or a [[stone#Noun|stone]]) that [[land#Verb|lands]] in the {{l|en|house|t=[[circular#Adjective|circular]] [[target#Noun|target]]}}. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
### {{lb|en|golf}} To hit (the ball) with the [[toe#Noun|toe]] of the [[club#Noun|club]] so that it is deflected toward the [[left#Noun|left]] (or, for a [[left-handed#Adjective|left-handed]] [[player]], toward the [[right#Noun|right]], originally in an [[uncontrolled]] and now a [[controlled#Adjective|controlled]] manner. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
###* {{quote-book|en|year=2013|author=Nick Bradley|title=Kinetic Golf|passage={{...}} the golfer thinks, “Ah! To '''draw''' the ball, I must be like the old pros and incorporate hand action through impact, twisting the clubhead to the left.” All you have to do to apply the spin needed to '''draw''' the ball left is to create a very small conflict between the clubface aim and the path the clubhead is traveling.}}
### {{lb|en|nautical}} Of a [[vessel#Noun|vessel]]: to [[require]] (a certain [[depth]] of [[water#Noun|water]]) to [[float#Verb|float]] in. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
###: {{ux|en|This ship '''draws''' ten feet of water.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|historical}}
### To drag (someone) by [[tie#Verb|tying]] behind a [[horse#Noun|horse]] or on a [[frame#Noun|frame]] as a [[form#Noun|form]] of [[punishment]] or [[torture#Noun|torture]], or to [[bring#Verb|bring]] to a place of [[execution]].
###* {{RQ:Grafton Chronicle|volume=II|chapter=[[w:Richard II of England|Richarde the Seconde]]|page=351|passage=And if any manner of perſon attempted to moue the King to infringe any parte of thys ordynaunce, and that being knowne, for the firſt time, he ſhould be depriued of his goodes and poſſeſſions, and for the ſecond time, to be '''drawen''' thorough the Citie, and ſo put to execution as an arrant traytor.}}
### To [[kill#Verb|kill]] someone as a form of punishment or torture by [[tear#Verb|tearing]] apart (their body) by tying their limbs to horses which [[run#Verb|run]] in [[different#Adjective|different]] directions; also, to tear (the limbs) from someone's body in this manner.
## {{lb|en|transitive|archery}} To pull back (an [[arrow#Noun|arrow]] or [[bowstring]]) in [[preparation]] for [[shoot#Verb|shooting]] the arrow; also, to cause (a [[bow#Noun|bow]]) to [[bend#Verb|bend]] by pulling back the bowstring. {{defdate|from 12th c.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|arithmetic}} To [[subject#Verb|subject]] (a [[number#Noun|number]]) to an [[arithmetic operation]].
## {{lb|en|transitive|fishing}} to [[haul#Verb|haul]] in (a [[fishing net]]) which has been [[cast#Verb|cast]]; also, to drag (a fishing net) alongside a [[boat#Noun|boat]].
## {{lb|en|transitive|nautical|archaic}} To [[hoist#Verb|hoist]] (a [[sail#Noun|sail]]).
## {{lb|en|transitive|UK|regional}} To [[carry#Verb|carry]] (a [[load#Noun|load]]) in a vehicle; to [[cart#Verb|cart]], to haul.
## {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}}
### To [[sew#Verb|sew]] together (the [[edge#Noun|edges]] of a [[tear#Noun|tear]]); also, to [[mend#Verb|mend]] (a [[hole#Noun|hole]] or tear) in [[fabric#Noun|fabric]].
### To [[use#Verb|use]] (a draught animal) to pull a plough or vehicle.
### {{lb|en|figurative}} To [[attribute#Verb|attribute]] (something) to a person or thing; to [[ascribe]].
### {{lb|en|figurative}} To [[change#Verb|change]] (something) into another thing; to [[convert#Verb|convert]], to [[transform#Verb|transform]].
### {{lb|en|figurative}} To [[convert#Verb|convert]] (a [[text#Noun|passage]]) from one [[literary]] [[style#Noun|style]] to another (for example, from [[prose#Noun|prose]] to [[verse#Noun|verse]]); or to [[translate#Verb|translate]] (a text) from one [[language#Noun|language]] to another.
### {{lb|en|figurative}} To [[direct#Verb|direct]] (one's [[heart#Noun|heart]], [[thought#Noun|thoughts]], etc.) in a certain [[way#Noun|way]] or towards someone or something.
### {{lb|en|figurative}} To [[give#Verb|give]] ([[courage#Noun|courage]], [[strength#Noun|strength]], etc.) to oneself; to [[summon up]]; also, to produce ([[evil#Noun|evil]], [[wickedness]], etc.) in oneself.
### {{lb|en|figurative}} To give (words) a certain [[meaning#Noun|meaning]], especially one which is [[distorted#Adjective|distorted]]; to [[distort#Verb|distort]], to [[misrepresent]].
### {{lb|en|figurative|cooking}} ''Followed by'' '''[[through#Preposition|through]]''': to [[pass#Verb|pass]] ([[food]]) through a [[strainer]].
### {{lb|en|figurative|cooking}} To [[mix#Verb|mix]] (an [[ingredient]]) with another ingredient or ingredients to form a [[liquid#Noun|liquid]] or [[paste#Noun|paste]]; to mix (ingredients) together to form a liquid or paste.
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To be ([[able#Adjective|able]] to be) pulled in a particular direction or manner.
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} Of [[blind#Noun|blinds]], a [[curtain#Noun|curtain]], etc.: to be pulled [[open#Adjective|open]] or [[closed#Adjective|closed]].
## {{lb|en|intransitive|figurative}}
### {{lb|en|bowls}} Of a bowl: to move in a curve to a certain place.
### {{lb|en|curling}} To make a shot that lands in the house.
### {{lb|en|nautical}} ''Followed by an [[adverb]], such as'' '''[[deep#Adverb|deep]]''' ''or'' '''[[shallow#Adverb|shallow]]''': of a [[vessel#Noun|vessel]]: to require a depth of water of a certain [[characteristic#Noun|characteristic]] to [[float#Verb|float]] in.
###* {{RQ:Shakespeare Troilus and Cressida|act=II|scene=iii|passage=Greater hulks '''draw''' deep.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive|archaic}}
### Especially of a [[draught animal]]: to pull something, such as a [[plough#Noun|plough]] or [[vehicle#Noun|vehicle]], along; to have force to move something by pulling. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
###: {{ux|en|This horse '''draws''' well.}}
### Of a plough or vehicle: to be pulled along in a specified manner. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
###: {{ux|en|The carriage '''draws''' easily.}}
### To [[become]] [[contracted#Adjective|contracted]]; to [[shrink#Verb|shrink]]. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
###* {{RQ:Bacon Sylva Sylvarum|passage=water{{...}} will shrink or '''draw''' into less room}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive|archery}} To pull back an arrow or bowstring in preparation for shooting the arrow; also, to cause a bow to bend by pulling back the bowstring.
## {{lb|en|intransitive|Scotland|figurative|archaic}} To [[work#Verb|work]] together towards a [[common#Adjective|common]] [[aim#Noun|aim]]; to [[cooperate]], to [[pull together]]; also, to have a [[good#Adjective|good]] [[relationship]] with; to [[get on with]].
## {{lb|en|intransitive|obsolete}}
### To be dragged along; to drag.
### To pull at something; to [[tug#Verb|tug]].
###* {{quote-book|en|year=1913|author=[[w:Robert Barr (writer)|Robert Barr]]|chapter=4|title=Lord Stranleigh Abroad|passage={{...}} No rogue e'er felt the halter {{quote-gloss|''i.e.'', the hangman's noose}} '''draw''', with a good opinion of the law, and perhaps my own detestation of the law arises from my having frequently broken it.}}
# {{non-gloss|Senses relating to [[attract]]ing.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[induce]] (the [[attention]], the [[eye#Noun|eyes]] or [[mind#Noun|mind]], etc.) to be [[direct#Verb|directed]] at or [[focus#Verb|focused]] on something. {{defdate|from 9th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|From the moment she entered the room, all eyes were '''drawn''' to her.}}
##: {{ux|en|His mind was '''drawn''' back to the events of the preceding morning.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|year=1964|month=April|title=Letters: Rethinking emergency procedures|journal=Modern Railways|page=274|passage=Handsignalmen, where needed, ought to wear a conspicuous orange/yellow cape (like many road workmen) to '''draw''' attention to them.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To attract or cause (someone) to [[come#Verb|come]] to a particular place or to [[take#Verb|take]] a particular [[course of action]]; also, to cause (someone) to [[turn away]] from a particular [[condition#Noun|condition]] or course of action. {{defdate|from 12th c.}}
##* {{RQ:Lincoln Pratt's Patients|chapter=5|passage=A place like the Right Livers' Rest was bound to '''draw''' freaks, same as molasses '''draws''' flies.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1935|author=George Goodchild|title=Death on the Centre Court|chapter=5|passage=The restaurant was packed, and the promenade between the two main courts and the subsidiary courts was thronged with healthy-looking youngish people, '''drawn''' to the Mecca of tennis from all parts of the country.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To attract (something) [[by means of]] a [[physical#Adjective|physical]] [[force#Noun|force]], especially [[gravity]] or [[magnetism]]. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
##* {{RQ:Bacon Sylva Sylvarum|passage=These following bodies do not '''draw''': smaragd, achates, corneolus, pearl, jaspis, chalcedonius, alabaster, porphyry, coral, marble, touchstone, haematites, or bloodstone {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Spectator|II|128|27 July 1711|passage=Keep a watch upon the particular bias which nature has fixed in their minds, that it may not '''draw''' too much.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To attract or [[provoke]] (a particular [[reaction]] or [[response]]) from someone. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|The president’s comments have '''drawn''' strong criticism from right-wing media outlets.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To cause (something) to [[occur]] as a [[consequence#Noun|consequence]]; to [[bring about]]. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=July 3, 2011|author=Piers Newbury|work=BBC Sport|title=[http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/tennis/14006575.stm Wimbledon 2011: Novak Djokovic beats Rafael Nadal in final]|passage=In a desperately tight opening set, the pace and accuracy of the Serbian's groundstrokes began to '''draw''' errors from the usually faultless Nadal and earned him the first break point of the day at 5-4.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} ''Followed by'' '''on''' ''or'' '''upon''': to bring ([[disaster]] or [[misfortune]]) on oneself.
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[receive#Verb|receive]] (a particular [[prison#Noun|prison]] [[sentence#Noun|sentence]]).
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To take ([[air#Noun|air]], [[smoke#Noun|smoke]], etc.) into the [[lung]]s; to [[breathe in]], to [[inhale]]. {{defdate|from 13th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|I '''drew''' a deep breath and wiped my brow.}}
##* {{RQ:Hough Purchase Price|chapter=I|passage=Serene, smiling, enigmatic, she faced him with no fear whatever showing in her dark eyes. {{...}} She put back a truant curl from her forehead where it had sought egress to the world, and looked him full in the face now, '''drawing''' a deep breath which caused the round of her bosom to lift the lace at her throat.}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1979|author=w:Monty Python|title=w:Always Look on the Bright Side of Life|passage=So always look on the bright side of death, / Just before you '''draw''' your terminal breath.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To cause (air) to be [[suck#Verb|sucked]] into a [[duct#Noun|duct]], a [[room#Noun|room]], etc.
## {{lb|en|transitive|archaic}} To [[drag#Verb|drag]] or suck [[deeply]] on (a [[cigarette]], [[pipe#Noun|pipe]], or other [[smoking#Noun|smoking]] [[implement#Noun|implement]]).
## {{lb|en|transitive|originally|and|chiefly|military}} To attract or provoke [[gunfire]], either [[intentionally]] or [[unintentionally]].
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To attract or [[influence#Verb|influence]] a person or group of people; to be an [[inducement]] or [[enticement]].
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To take a [[drink#Noun|drink]] of a [[beverage]], especially an [[alcoholic#Adjective|alcoholic]] one; to [[swig#Verb|swig]].
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} Of a duct, smoking implement, etc.: to [[allow]] air to be passed through it in order that [[combustion]] can occur. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|The chimney won’t '''draw''' properly if it’s clogged up with soot.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} ''Followed by'' '''[[at#Preposition|at]]''' ''or'' '''on''': to drag or suck deeply on a cigarette, pipe, or other smoking implement.
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} ''Chiefly followed by'' '''[[about#Adverb|about]]''' ''or'' '''[[around#Adverb|around]]''': of a group of people: to [[come together]]; to [[assemble]], to [[congregate#Verb|congregate]], to [[gather#Verb|gather]].
# {{non-gloss|Senses relating to [[extend#Verb|extending]] or [[protract]]ing.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To make (something) [[large#Adjective|larger]] or [[long#Adjective|longer]]; to [[elongate#Verb|elongate]], to stretch. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|The dough was run through the pasta machine and '''drawn''' into a long ribbon.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} ''Followed by'' '''[[out#Preposition|out]]''': to [[flatten]] (a [[piece#Noun|piece]] of [[metal#Noun|metal]]), usually by [[hammer#Verb|hammering]].
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To make ([[wire#Noun|wire]]) by pulling a [[rod#Noun|rod]] or other piece of metal through one or more [[aperture]]s; also, to [[stretch#Verb|stretch]] (a rod or other piece of metal) into a wire. {{defdate|from 13th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|to '''draw''' a mass of metal into wire}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To make ([[straw#Noun|straw]] [[straight#Adjective|straight]] for [[thatch#Verb|thatching]] by pulling it through the [[hand#Noun|hands]].
## {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}}
### To [[amount#Verb|amount]] to (a certain [[quantity]] or [[sum#Noun|sum]]).
### To extend the [[duration]] of (something); to [[prolong]]. {{defdate|10th|19th c.}}
### To stretch (someone) on a [[rack#Noun|rack]] as a form of punishment or torture.
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To be made larger or longer; to be elongated or stretched.
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To make straw straight for thatching by pulling it through the hands.
## {{lb|en|intransitive|nautical}} Of a sail: to [[fill#Verb|fill]] with [[wind#Noun|wind]] and become [[taut#Adjective|taut]]. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|The ship’s sail '''drew''' when the wind blew strongly.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive|obsolete}}
### To amount to a certain quantity or sum.
### To extend in [[area]] or [[space#Noun|space]]; to [[spread#Verb|spread]], to stretch.
# {{non-gloss|Senses relating to [[extract#Verb|extracting]] or [[select#Verb|selecting]].}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To pull (something) out; to extract, to [[remove#Verb|remove]].
### To extract (a [[tooth#Noun|tooth]]); to pull. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
### To extract ([[juice#Noun|juice]], [[oil#Noun|oil]], or some other [[fluid#Noun|fluid]]) from something by [[osmosis]], [[pressure#Noun|pressure]], or another [[process#Noun|process]]. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
###* {{quote-text|en|year=1705|author={{w|George Cheyne (physician)|George Cheyne}}|title=Philosophical Principles of Religion Natural and Revealed|passage=Spirits, by distillations, may be '''drawn''' out of vegetable juices, which shall flame and fume of themselves.}}
### To extract (a [[small#Adjective|small]] [[amount#Noun|amount]] of liquid, especially [[blood#Noun|blood]]) by [[puncture#Verb|puncturing]] a surface, or by using a [[pipette#Noun|pipette]], [[syringe#Noun|syringe]], or other [[suction#Noun|suction]] [[device]].
###: {{ux|en|The cat scratched her, '''drawing''' blood.}}
### To [[leave#Verb|leave]] ([[tea#Noun|tea]]) [[temporarily]] in water to allow the [[flavor#Noun|flavour]] to [[increase#Verb|increase]]; to [[infuse]], to [[steep#Verb|steep]]. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
### To pull out (a [[firearm]], [[sword#Noun|sword]], or other [[weapon#Noun|weapon]]) from a [[holster#Noun|holster]], [[sheath#Noun|sheath]], etc.; to [[unsheathe]]. {{defdate|from 12th c.}}
###: {{ux|en|They '''drew''' their swords and fought each other.}}
